{"archetypes":[{"name":"shell-robustness","focus_area":"code-robustness","weight":1,"rationale":"New check-reviewers.sh adds background PIDs, EXIT trap cleanup, SECONDS-global-reset for timeout, and mktemp+mv atomicity — all concurrent-lifecycle patterns not well-covered by static correctness/edge-cases reviewers.","prompt_body":"Scrutinize the new background-probe mechanics in `scripts/check-reviewers.sh`: the PROBE_PIDS / PROBE_TMPFILES EXIT-trap cleanup vs active probes (race between trap firing and still-running PID), the `SECONDS=0` global reset inside `larch_poll_probe_pid` (shared builtin — what happens if the function is called while another shell timer depends on it), the `kill -0` + `wait` TOCTOU window, and the dual `stat -c %Y` / `stat -f %m` mtime fallback. Also verify that `larch_write_bool_stamp` (mktemp + mv) is actually atomic on macOS HFS+ / APFS (same-filesystem mv requirement). Verify that `AUTH_ATTEMPT` and `CURSOR_AUTH_ARGS` being read from outer scope inside `larch_run_one_cursor_probe` / `larch_run_one_codex_probe` is safe under all exit paths (early return on mktemp failure, mutex acquire failure). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"session-passthrough","focus_area":"correctness","weight":1,"rationale":"session-setup.sh now infers CODEX_BINARY_FOUND/CURSOR_BINARY_FOUND from CALLER_CODEX_PRESENT when the caller-env lacks the new keys — this inference equates probe-result with binary-found, which is semantically wrong for probe-failed=false cases.","prompt_body":"Examine the non-probe passthrough branch in `scripts/session-setup.sh` (the `else` block after `if [[ \"$CHECK_REVIEWERS\" == \"true\" ]]`): when `CALLER_CODEX_BINARY_FOUND` is absent but `CALLER_CODEX_PRESENT=false`, the code infers `_passthrough_codex_bin=false` and emits `CODEX_BINARY_FOUND=false` — but `CODEX_PRESENT=false` could mean probe-failed (binary present, probe unhealthy), not binary absent. Check whether this inference is correct or could confuse downstream consumers (e.g., `SKILL.md`'s two-tier warning logic that keys on `CODEX_BINARY_FOUND=false` vs `CODEX_PRESENT=false`). Also verify that `FINAL_CODEX_BINARY_FOUND` / `FINAL_CURSOR_BINARY_FOUND` are correctly populated and passed to `write-session-env.sh` in both the probe and passthrough branches, and that `write-session-env.sh` content-building appends the new keys in the right order relative to the `*_PRESENT` and `*_AVAILABLE` keys. Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."},{"name":"bash32-compat","focus_area":"architecture","weight":1,"rationale":"BASH_AUTHORING.md mandates Bash 3.2 compatibility for committed scripts; check-reviewers.sh now uses C-style for loops, printf -v, and array append patterns that need explicit verification.","prompt_body":"Audit every new or modified shell construct in `scripts/check-reviewers.sh` and `scripts/test-check-reviewers.sh` against the Bash 3.2 compatibility matrix from BASH_AUTHORING.md: confirm that `for ((i = 0; i < N; i++))` C-style loops, `printf -v varname`, `PROBE_TMPFILES[${#PROBE_TMPFILES[@]}]=value` array-append-by-index, `${arr[@]+\"${arr[@]}\"}` empty-array guard, and `(( arithmetic ))` are all valid in Bash 3.2. Also check whether any `[[` tests use regex (`=~`) with capture groups or features unavailable in 3.2, and whether the `SECONDS` built-in assignment `SECONDS=0` inside a function is supported in Bash 3.2 (it is a special variable but confirm there is no caveats). Cite specific file paths and line ranges for any issues found, and follow the output-format rules from your outer wrapper exactly."}]}
